  Pure. No [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m, no [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no DOM — all three injected. That's what makes "does a task due in 23 hours
  count as due-soon, and does it flip at 25?" a three-line test instead of a fake-timer exercise. Returns the
  undismissed set sorted by [38;5;153mdueDate[39m ascending.

  [1mDismissals.[0m A persisted [38;5;153mstring[][39m of keys, pruned on write to drop keys whose [38;5;153mtaskId[39m no longer exists — otherwise it
  grows forever as tasks are deleted.

  The composite key is doing real work: because [38;5;153mdueDate[39m is [3min[0m the key, dismissing "due soon" and then rescheduling the
  task produces a different key, so you get notified again for the new deadline. A plain notification ID would silently
  stay dismissed — the wrong behavior, and an easy bug to ship.

  [1mStorage.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, versioned keys ([38;5;153mtasks.v1[39m, [38;5;153mdismissed.v1[39m), behind a small module so the future swap to a server
  touches one file.

